atients with inflammatory bowel disease, where it reflects their nutritional status. The relationship between prealbumina and albumina highlights the interconnectedness of these proteins in nutritional assessments [Data: Entities (1732); Relationships (3277, 6982, 7419, 7330, 7418, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The Maastricht Index as a comprehensive assessment tool\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Maastricht Index is a prognostic index that utilizes various nutritional risk markers, including albumina and prealbumina, to assess nutritional status. This index is particularly useful in clinical settings for identifying patients at risk of malnutrition. Its application in evaluating nutritional health underscores the importance of these proteins in comprehensive assessments, especially in populations experiencing undernutrition [Data: Entities (4801); Relationships (710, 948, 7395, 7418, 14063, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"BIA Multifrequency's contribution to body composition analysis\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Multifrequency bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA) is a method used to estimate body water and composition, which is particularly useful in clinical settings. This technique can help monitor changes in body composition related to conditions such as sarcopenia and edematous states. The integration of BIA with nutritional assessments enhances the understanding of a patient's overall health and nutritional status, making it a valuable tool in managing chronic conditions [Data: Entities (4802); Relationships (1690, 14066, 14064, +more)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"DOPPS study's insights into dialysis patients\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Dialysis Outcomes and Practice Patterns Study (DOPPS) evaluates the outcomes of dialysis patients, including their nutritional status across various demographics. This study confirms the prognostic value of albumina